WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.910
Hello and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.830
And today we're going to be
looking at reduction reactions

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:15.939
of aldehydes and ketones for
introductory organic chemistry.

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:20.019
So what we're going to be specifically
looking at is aldehydes and ketones

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.430
are reduced by the addition of hydrogen
in the presence of a metal catalyst.

00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:30.289
Meaning that when, when we say that
word reduced, what we want to be able

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:36.190
to think about is reducing the number
of oxygens And add, adding hydrogens.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:41.620
So if I have, let's say the following
example here, where what, what we're going

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.720
to be doing is reducing the number of CO
bonds by essentially opening the doors

00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:48.820
like how we did in an addition reaction.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:52.900
So simply, we're going to be
taking our aldehydes and ketones

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.430
and turning them into alcohols.

00:00:55.925 --> 00:00:59.395
So just by showing, let's say, a
little example like this, right,

00:00:59.395 --> 00:01:04.115
we're changing the double bond to
an alcohol, so reducing CO bonds.

00:01:04.405 --> 00:01:06.765
So in this video, we're going to
go through mechanisms, trying to

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:10.885
understand that entire reduction
process for us to, and going

00:01:10.885 --> 00:01:12.644
through multiple examples of that.

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:16.285
So please stay tuned and let's
go into how this reaction works.

00:01:21.795 --> 00:01:25.124
Okay, so the basic
mechanism is just like this.

00:01:25.244 --> 00:01:27.515
So it's similar to an addition reaction.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.460
Or if you remember from that
previous video, it was all about

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.880
trying to open up our double doors.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.039
So meaning if I have a double bond, CO
double bond, we're going to be opening

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:39.449
it up just like a little cabinet.

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:43.890
And when I open that up, we're going
to be adding something to each part.

00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:48.380
So if I have my bond here, the way
that this is going to be shown is that

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.890
we're going to have a reducing agent.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:57.270
That is going to be changing this
particular and causing this reaction.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.929
So typically it's written with an H.

00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:03.339
Sometimes you might see it written
with an R as well to represent, hey,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.720
this is going through reduction.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.669
There will typically be some
kind of catalyst as well.

00:02:08.799 --> 00:02:11.380
Depending on your class, every
teacher is a little different.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.160
They might try to tell you
exactly how much you need to know.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.140
So, if we redraw our carbon
structure, the only thing that is

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.580
changing is our functional groups.

00:02:22.065 --> 00:02:24.895
But we are also adding two components.

00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:28.055
So, as I mentioned, we're going
to be opening up the doors.

00:02:28.055 --> 00:02:29.785
So, that is our double bond here.

00:02:29.785 --> 00:02:31.444
Notice how we're opening it up.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:36.044
And what we're going to be doing
here is, as we open up those doors,

00:02:36.285 --> 00:02:38.605
we are adding hydrogen, right?

00:02:38.605 --> 00:02:42.125
So, we're adding hydrogens
to the double bond, uh, just

00:02:42.125 --> 00:02:43.735
like I have written up here.

00:02:44.315 --> 00:02:46.895
So, when we open that, we
can see that, okay, here's a

00:02:46.895 --> 00:02:49.165
hydrogen on each individual part.

00:02:49.614 --> 00:02:53.575
So we have on the left hand
side an aldehyde turning into a

00:02:53.584 --> 00:02:56.274
primary alcohol when that happens.

00:02:56.705 --> 00:03:02.465
Okay, so aldehyde goes towards a
primary alcohol, and that is for

00:03:02.495 --> 00:03:04.555
this particular reduction example.

00:03:05.114 --> 00:03:10.545
Let's go on to, into both of these looking
at aldehydes and ketones specifically.

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:15.105
So like we were just doing previously,
you can see that an aldehyde, we are

00:03:15.145 --> 00:03:19.585
opening up those doors and we're adding
on the hydrogens onto each component.

00:03:19.994 --> 00:03:23.834
So an aldehyde is always going
to turn into a primary alcohol.

00:03:24.415 --> 00:03:26.584
Now what about ketones?

00:03:26.585 --> 00:03:28.174
Well ketones can also reduce as well.

00:03:28.494 --> 00:03:32.715
So a ketone, being that it's in the
middle of the chain, this is going

00:03:32.715 --> 00:03:34.825
to turn into a secondary alcohol.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:39.020
It's going to be the same general
reaction though, we're going to

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.540
be opening up those double doors
on our carbon carbon double bond.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:49.380
So what we're going to do is take
our CO, we're going to just slightly

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:54.889
move around our R groups just to help
make room for our cabinets to open.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.739
We'll put a door on each carbon and
oxygen and what we're going to be

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:02.810
doing is adding a hydrogen to each one.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:07.845
So as you can see, we go to a secondary
alcohol versus primary just to kind of

00:04:07.845 --> 00:04:11.334
review what that means in case maybe you
don't remember from organic chemistry

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.935
or previous weeks in your courses.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.029
So the whole idea is that, okay, when we
have, let's go to the primary example,

00:04:17.409 --> 00:04:21.809
is if we go to this carbon that's
attached to the alcohol, you're going

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:27.640
to count how many R groups there are
next to the carbon with your alcohol.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.330
So what I usually do is put a little star.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.180
And I count how many R
groups there are next to it.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:38.400
So this top one is a primary versus this
one we star the carbon with the alcohol

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:44.430
and we see one and then two is how we
would look at this particular reduction.

00:04:44.899 --> 00:04:48.440
So primary and secondary
alcohols can reduce.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.909
Sometimes you can also see where a
carboxylic acid can reduce as well,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.200
where that can go to an aldehyde and
then to a primary alcohol as well.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.129
Let's go through a bunch of
examples and try to understand

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.629
and review this process together.

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:07.030
So, in a lot of your chemistry classes,
the way that these are going to be

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:11.220
presented to you is to predict the
products of the following reaction,

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:15.340
which is a common exam, quiz,
homework problem that you'll see in an

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.590
introductory organic chemistry course.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.380
So, for this one, what we have for the
very first one, we can see, we, the first

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.309
thing we have to do is identify what is
our functional group, and so that way we

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:29.979
have an idea of, well, what do we expect?

00:05:30.289 --> 00:05:34.530
Well, we can see that we have the C double
bond O, and it's in the middle of a chain.

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.039
So whenever it is in the middle,
this is our ketone, right?

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:41.729
So in this case, what
does the ketone turn into?

00:05:41.969 --> 00:05:44.929
Well, if you can remember how is
the functional group changing, it

00:05:44.929 --> 00:05:48.939
can help guide your thinking and how
exactly to pick your final answer.

00:05:49.319 --> 00:05:52.559
So ketone is going to
go to a primary alcohol.

00:05:53.205 --> 00:05:57.505
So when we do this, remember from
before we're going to be opening up

00:05:57.555 --> 00:06:00.245
the double door on our double bond.

00:06:00.245 --> 00:06:05.195
So when that opens, we're adding a
hydrogen to each individual part.

00:06:05.205 --> 00:06:08.254
So let's redraw our structure, right?

00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:12.029
So keeping the base
compound portions here.

00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:16.929
And then now we're going to draw
our doors on the carbon that this

00:06:16.979 --> 00:06:22.319
O is connected to as well as the,
the carbon and the oxygen, right?

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:25.469
And now what was the whole idea
with reduction is we're adding

00:06:25.479 --> 00:06:28.409
a hydrogen to each component.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.560
So just like this, we have now
an extra hydrogen on each part.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.999
So just to kind of clean that up for
ourselves, because it looks a little

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.220
wonky, it's going to be the same
thing as saying, hey, we have this 2-

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:48.630
propanol is what is going to be created
from the reduction of 2- propanone.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.419
So it goes from 2-
propanone to 2- propanol.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:58.210
Now, with the next one, alright, well
it's the same general idea, right?

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:02.559
We have a benzaldehyde
here, so benzaldehyde.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.300
How will that go through
a reduction oxidation?

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:09.370
We want to go through that entire
little checklist for ourselves.

00:07:09.864 --> 00:07:13.195
So being that it's an aldehyde, a
double bond, it can reduce, right?

00:07:13.195 --> 00:07:17.395
So same thing as before, we're
going to be essentially opening

00:07:17.455 --> 00:07:18.844
